<BaseLayout title="GB20 (Fengchi) — Wind Pool Acupressure Point" description="Learn how to locate and stimulate GB20 (Fengchi) — the Wind Pool point. Benefits include headache relief, neck tension relief, and sinus support.">
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6">
    <h1 class="mb-4">GB20 (Fengchi) — Wind Pool</h1>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">GB20, known as Fengchi or Wind Pool, is located at the base of the skull and is a primary point for headaches, neck tension, sinus congestion, and common cold symptoms.</p>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Location</h2>
    <img src="/images/gb20-location.png" alt="GB20 acupressure point location at the base of the skull" class="w-full max-w-sm mx-auto rounded-xl shadow-sm" />
    <p class="mb-3">GB20 is in the hollows at the base of the skull, between the two large neck muscles (sternocleidomastoid and trapezius), approximately 2 cun apart from each other. They lie on the Gallbladder meridian.</p>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Benefits &amp; Uses</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li><strong>Headaches and migraines:</strong> GB20 is highly effective for tension headaches, migraines, and occipital headaches.</li>
      <li><strong>Neck tension and stiffness:</strong> It releases tightness in the neck and upper shoulders, improving range of motion.</li>
      <li><strong>Sinus congestion:</strong> GB20 helps relieve sinus pressure and stuffiness associated with allergies and colds.</li>
      <li><strong>Eye strain:</strong> It alleviates eye fatigue, blurry vision, and headaches from prolonged screen use.</li>
      <li><strong>Common cold:</strong> It is a key point for expelling "wind" in traditional Chinese medicine and easing early cold symptoms.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">How to Massage GB20</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Place your thumbs on the hollows at the base of your skull.</li>
      <li>Apply firm upward pressure with your thumbs in small circular motions.</li>
      <li>Keep your head slightly dropped forward to access the point more easily.</li>
      <li>Hold for 1–2 minutes while breathing deeply.</li>
      <li>Repeat as needed throughout the day.</li>
    </ol>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Science &amp; Research</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">A 2019 systematic review in <em>BMJ Open</em> found that GB20 acupuncture (and acupressure) was effective for migraine prophylaxis and reducing headache frequency.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31467087/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1</a></sup> Research in <em>Cephalalgia</em> demonstrated that GB20 stimulation reduced blood flow velocity in the posterior cerebral artery, correlating with headache relief.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26024974/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2</a></sup></p>
    <p class="mb-3">A 2022 study in <em>Frontiers in Neurology</em> showed that GB20 massage combined with standard care significantly reduced neck pain and disability in chronic neck pain patients.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36388202/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3</a></sup></p>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Precautions</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Use gentle pressure — the base of the skull is sensitive and pressing too hard can cause dizziness.</li>
      <li>Avoid if you have a recent neck injury, cervical spine instability, or concussion.</li>
      <li>Do not press on both GB20 points simultaneously with extreme force.</li>
      <li>Stop if you feel lightheaded or nauseous.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Related Points</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">GB20 pairs well with <a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point/">LI4 (Hegu)</a> for headache relief and with <a href="/points/gb21-jianjing-acupressure-point/">GB21 (Jianjing)</a> for neck and shoulder tension. For sinus issues, combine with <a href="/points/li11-quchi-acupressure-point/">LI11 (Quchi)</a>.</p>
  </section></article>
</BaseLayout>
